WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.872
A random variable turns an uncertain outcome into a numerical value. Its distribution lists probability mass

00:00:06.872 --> 00:00:13.543
for discrete outcomes or assigns a density across a continuous domain. A density height can exceed

00:00:13.543 --> 00:00:18.130
one because interval area, rather than height alone, is a probability.

00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:25.286
A random variable assigns a number to an uncertain outcome. Its distribution says how much probability

00:00:25.286 --> 00:00:26.533
each value receives.

00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:34.053
The two probabilities are nonnegative and sum to one. These are probability masses, not continuous densities.

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:40.369
Must the expected value be one of the variable’s possible outcomes? Pause here and make a

00:00:40.369 --> 00:00:41.457
prediction before continuing.

00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:50.190
The expectation need not be a possible single outcome. For example, an expectation of one-half does

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:57.814
not mean a Bernoulli trial returns one-half. For our starting inputs, Mass at zero: 0.4. Mass

00:00:57.814 --> 00:01:00.197
at one: 0.6. Expectation: 0.6.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:07.304
The sample mean is the observed fraction of ones. For independent identically distributed trials it concentrates

00:01:07.304 --> 00:01:12.670
around p as the sample size grows, while any finite sample can differ.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:19.920
Distinguish a random variable, one realized value, a distribution and an estimator of its expectation.

